      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to the Earth and to living things, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The States of Water\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct forms (states of matter) within normal planetary temperatures:\n*   **Liquid:** The flowing water we drink, swim in, and find in rivers, lakes, and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, and steam rising from boiling water.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique physical properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through our bodies and through ecosystems.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different; when it freezes, its molecules form a crystalline structure that actually takes up *more* space. This means ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps human body temperatures stable.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up from the roots of a tree to its highest leaves).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:**"
